<div class="page">
  <div class="section-title">ACADEMIC KNOWLEDGE AND SKILLS</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    Students need to acquire core academic knowledge and skills in order to be college ready and to experience college success. This is traditionally considered the predominant factor predicting future academic performance (Camara & Echternacht 2000; DeBerard, Spielmans, & Julka 2004), though it is not the sole factor. Core knowledge and skills include grasping key content and engaging in critical thinking, writing, and argumentation. We propose that the following academic experiences and attainments in high school contribute to college readiness and success:
  </div>

  <div class="sub-heading">EXPERIENCE: PARTICIPATING IN A RIGOROUS CORE CURRICULUM IN HIGH SCHOOL</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    <span data-field="research_base_1">{{ research_base_1 }}</span> Adelman's (2006) often-cited research found that the academic intensity of students' high school coursework was the most important factor in their momentum toward a Bachelor's degree. "Academic intensity" refers to participation in a substantial number of challenging high school courses. The highest level of math is particularly important, with courses above Algebra II (Pre-calculus/Trigonometry, Calculus) considered to be "tipping points" in predicting student success in college.
    <br><br>
    Similarly, ACT, known widely for its college admissions test, has done research for a number of years on the courses students need to become college ready. The studies have compared high school students who completed a rigorous core of college-preparatory courses with peers who did not do so. Students who completed the college-
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="sub-heading">HIGH SCHOOL AND COLLEGE COLLABORATIVE WORK</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    <span data-field="research_base_2">{{ research_base_2 }}</span> preparatory core courses were 12 percent more likely to enroll in college, 9 percent more likely to achieve a first-year college grade point average (GPA) of 3.0 or higher, and 8 percent more likely to earn a college degree (ACT, Inc. 2007).
    <br><br>
    <span data-field="research_base_3">{{ research_base_3 }}</span> Research indicates that students generally benefit from taking challenging courses, even when they have not performed well academically in the past (Boroch & Hope 2009).
    <br><br>
    <span data-field="research_base_4">{{ research_base_4 }}</span> High school and college collaborative work
    <br>
    Colleges can play a key role in informing schools, students, and parents about the importance of taking rigorous courses in high school. In these conversations, they can emphasize the advantages of fully utilizing the senior year to take advanced, college-preparatory courses (Boroch & Hope 2009). In addition, college and high school faculty can analyze the alignment between the courses offered in high school and the courses available at the college. As a part of this analysis process, colleges can provide feedback to high schools on how to make sure that courses provide high-quality preparation for college.
  </div>

  <div class="sub-heading">EXPERIENCE: TAKING COLLEGE-LEVEL COURSES WHILE IN HIGH SCHOOL</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    <span data-field="research_base_5">{{ research_base_5 }}</span> Participation in college-level courses in high school can serve as an on-ramp to postsecondary success, especially for traditionally underserved students. Dual enrollment
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="sub-heading">HIGH SCHOOL ROLE</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    <span data-field="high_school_role_1">{{ high_school_role_1 }}</span> programs allow students to take actual college courses while still in high school, sometimes for both high school and college credit. Karp and colleagues (2008) found that students who participated in dual enrollment were more likely to graduate high school and enroll in college than similar students who did not participate. In addition, dual enrollment participants were more likely to earn a higher GPA in high school and to accrue more college credits.
    <br><br>
    <span data-field="high_school_role_2">{{ high_school_role_2 }}</span> Similar results were found for New York City dual enrollment students (Karp et al. 2008). A study conducted in Texas also found substantially better college outcomes for students who took dual enrollment courses (Struth & Vargas 2012).
    <br><br>
    <span data-field="high_school_role_3">{{ high_school_role_3 }}</span> The research support for participation in Advanced Placement (AP) and International Baccalaureate (IB) courses is less robust. Both programs offer rigorous courses that include opportunities to demonstrate mastery on final exams, often resulting in the award of college credit. They also position students well for admission to selective colleges (Herberg-Davis, Callahan, & Kyburg 2006). However, a number of studies (Dougherty, Megler, & Jian 2005; Geisler & Santuces 2004; Klopfenstein & Thomas 2009) found little to no predictive value on college success for students who participated in AP courses, although some studies associated with positive outcomes in college (Geisler & Santuces 2004). One study showed that students who enrolled in IB programs went on to earn higher GPAs in college (Geisler & Santuces 2004).
  </div>

  <div class="sub-heading">HIGH SCHOOL AND COLLEGE COLLABORATIVE WORK</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    <span data-field="high_school_college_collaboration_1">{{ high_school_college_collaboration_1 }}</span> Colleges can work with high schools to provide access to a range of dual enrollment opportunities and to support students so that they successfully complete them. They can also provide data analyses showing how students perform in dual enrollment courses and whether courses will count toward specific programs of study at the college.
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="section-title">NONCOGNITIVE SKILLS</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    As noted above, success in college depends on more than just academic knowledge and skills. Noncognitive skills are also important for college success (Garcia 2014), as are school environments that support the development of these skills. These skills, which are often referred to as "noncognitive," refer to skills and qualities related to adjustment, motivation, and student perception. Sedlack (2011) and Levin (2012) argue that noncognitive skills are predictive of college success, and that they can be taught. Common examples of noncognitive skills include affective dispositions such as time management, perseverance, and goal setting; and nonacademic skills such as time management, perseverance, and goal setting.
    <br><br>
    We propose that the following noncognitive experiences and attainments in high school contribute to college readiness and success:
  </div>

  <div class="sub-heading">EXPERIENCE: OPPORTUNITIES TO DEVELOP PERSONAL GOALS AND LIFE DIRECTION</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    <span data-field="research_base_6">{{ research_base_6 }}</span> The opportunity to develop goals, both academic and occupational, is associated with a greater likelihood of entering and persisting in postsecondary education (Harciewicz, Barron, Tauer, & Elliot 2002; Terenzini, Cabrera, & Bernal 2001). In their report Swimming Against the Tide, Terenzini et al. (2001) note that, particularly for students from low socioeconomic status backgrounds, an early predisposition toward college enrollment is an important predictor of eventual college
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="sub-heading">HIGH SCHOOL ROLE</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    <span data-field="high_school_role_4">{{ high_school_role_4 }}</span> High schools should provide assistance to students in figuring out their educational and career goals, although the amount of time devoted to these visits varies. Light-touch ways of helping students with career/major selection include career inventories, college visits, and guidance counseling. More substantive assistance occurs in the context of career pathway programs (e.g., career academies, California's Linked Learning initiative), job experiences (e.g., internships, service learning, and co-ops), career-focused dual enrollment, and apprenticeships. However, working with colleges could strengthen all of these efforts.
  </div>

  <div class="sub-heading">HIGH SCHOOL AND COLLEGE COLLABORATIVE WORK</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    <span data-field="high_school_college_collaboration_2">{{ high_school_college_collaboration_2 }}</span> Colleges can provide information to high schools and students on the links between high school performance and college success. Ideally, this information could come from particular high schools who entered college with different GPAs. They can also offer clear information on the majors and career options that are likely to be open to students at each academic performance level.
  </div>

  <div class="sub-heading">ATTAINMENT: MATH AND ENGLISH FOUNDATIONAL KNOWLEDGE AT THE COLLEGE LEVEL</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    <span data-field="research_base_7">{{ research_base_7 }}</span> While participation in a variety of rigorous courses is important preparation for college, extra emphasis should be placed on acquiring foundational knowledge in math and English. High levels of literacy and numeracy are important for success in a range of disciplinary areas (Conley 2007). In addition, colleges often test for math and English knowledge and skills when students enroll and place them into remedial courses if they are not at the college level. Participation in remedial, or developmental, courses often results in lower rates of college persistence and completion (Jaggars & Stacey 2015).
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="sub-heading">ATTTAINMENT: ACCRUING SIX COLLEGE CREDITS DURING HIGH SCHOOL</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    <span data-field="research_base_8">{{ research_base_8 }}</span> It is not enough just to understand the value of a good GPA. Students need a range of supports—including tutoring, help with family and financial issues, and peer and adult mentoring.
  </div>

  <div class="sub-heading">HIGH SCHOOL ROLE</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    <span data-field="high_school_role_5">{{ high_school_role_5 }}</span> High schools should help students and parents understand the importance of maintaining a good GPA from the beginning of high school or even earlier. Students may not understand the longer-term implications of decisions they make regarding how much effort to devote to their studies (Boroch & Hope 2009). At the same time, it is not enough just to understand the value of a good GPA. Students need a range of supports—including supplemental instruction and tutoring, help with family and financial issues, and peer and adult mentoring.
  </div>

  <div class="sub-heading">HIGH SCHOOL AND COLLEGE COLLABORATIVE WORK</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    <span data-field="high_school_college_collaboration_3">{{ high_school_college_collaboration_3 }}</span> Colleges can provide information to high schools and students on the links between high school performance and college success. Ideally, this information could come from particular high schools who entered college with different GPAs. They can also offer clear information on the majors and career options that are likely to be open to students at each academic performance level.
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="sub-heading">HIGH SCHOOL ROLE</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    <span data-field="high_school_role_6">{{ high_school_role_6 }}</span> High schools can make sure that every student is able to take two college courses, typically worth six college credits, during high school. Alternatively, students can be encouraged to take AP and IB courses. Although they don't guarantee college credit, students who pass associated exams are generally awarded college credit.
  </div>

  <div class="sub-heading">HIGH SCHOOL AND COLLEGE COLLABORATIVE WORK</div>
  <div class="content-block">
    <span data-field="high_school_college_collaboration_4">{{ high_school_college_collaboration_4 }}</span> Colleges can work with high schools to provide access to a range of dual enrollment opportunities and to support students so that they successfully complete them. They can also provide data analyses showing how students perform in dual enrollment courses and whether courses will count toward specific programs of study at the college.
  </div>
</div>

</body>
